  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 229 PM EDT Tuesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Cool, dry, and breezy conditions continue across the \nregion this afternoon with temperatures generally in the upper 40s \nto mid 50s. West to northwesterly winds continue to be breezy, \nthough not as strong as yesterday. These winds will drop off this \nevening, which will allow for temperatures to drop this evening as \nideal radiational cooling takes place given the dry airmass across \nthe region. Temperatures overnight will generally drop into the 20s \nand 30s across the region. A Frost Advisory remains in effect for \nthe Champlain Valley, where the growing season is still active. The \nbest potential for frost development will be earlier in the night, \nwith southerly winds and increased cloud cover putting an end to \nfrost potential by 5AM or so. \n\nFor tomorrow, the region will be on the periphery of a low centered \nover James Bay, with an associated warm front passing through mid-\nday tomorrow. Temperatures tomorrow will be much warmer in \ncomparison to this afternoon, with daytime highs generally in the \nupper 50s and 60s. Some light rain showers will be possible \nthroughout the day tomorrow, particularly in the afternoon or \nevening but expected rainfall amounts will be fairly light. \nAdditional shower chances will be possible through the end of the \nwork week as a cold front moves through on Thursday. Thursday looks \nlike it could be quite similar to yesterday, with more convective \nshowers developing behind the front and the potential for some small \nhail and/or graupel.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Large ridging over the Mid-Atlantic states is expected \nto roll eastward supporting an increase in ridging over the \nNortheast. Conditions will be around to slightly below seasonal \naverages for Saturday as ridging keeps conditions dry. Sunday, flow \nis favored to shift more out of the south to southwest promoting \nwarm air advection and temperatures warming above seasonal averages \ninto the upper 60s to around 70 degrees. Winds will likely be on the \nrise under this pattern shift for Sunday with gusts 15-20 mph. \n\nBeyond Sunday, the high is favored to remain progressive with a \nsouthern trajectory low pressure system potentially riding up the \nbackside of the ridge. This low could be the remnants of a tropical \nsystem out of the Gulf waters off Texas and Louisiana. However, \nmodels tend to be too progressive with these features at this time \nscale, so it's more likely that this low will be delayed longer. If \nso, a northern stream trough will sweep through. Either way, some \nprecipitation chances are possible.",
